16. If they ask: "Why not just sort by priority?"

Answer:

"Because priority alone doesn't capture the business requirement. A ticket can breach its deadline, so overdue status needs to influence the queue. I therefore use multiple deterministic sorting criteria rather than a simple priority sort."

17. If they ask: "What happens when two tickets have the same priority?"

Answer:

"The ticket with the earlier deadline gets priority. If their deadlines are also equal, creation time is used as the final tie-breaker."

18. If they ask: "What happens if an URGENT ticket becomes overdue?"

Answer:

"It remains URGENT because URGENT is already the highest priority. It is still marked as overdue and remains at the front of the queue."

19. If they ask: "Can escalation happen multiple times?"

Answer:

"Yes, across separate escalation runs, provided the ticket remains overdue and active. But each run increases priority by only one level."

Example:

Run 1:
NORMAL → HIGH

Run 2:
HIGH → URGENT

Run 3:
URGENT → URGENT
